# DataRead DataEdit design

How a Fearless main reads and edits the 'resources' of a data project.
The code declares the types naming the data the code needs.
The user of the manager mounts a data project on each of those types, picking the data project from a menu.

The algorithms of this design specify the behaviour, not the implementation: an implementation may take the steps of an algorithm in another order, or in another way, when no person and no program can tell the difference, also after a crash.

### Terms

- 'data project': a registered project of kind `dataRead` or `dataEdit`.
- 'DataRead type': a type implementing `base.DataRead`. 'DataEdit type': a type implementing `base.DataEdit`. Every DataEdit type is also a DataRead type.
- 'mount': an entry of `mounts` in `projects.info` (see Registered projects in ControllersDesign): a data project mounted on a DataRead type of a project.
- 'broken mount': a mount `t -> n` of a project p with `broken(p, t -> n)`:
```
broken(p, t -> n)  iff  one of these holds:
  t is not a DataRead type of p
  n is not the name of a registered project
  n names a project of kind idle or code
  n names a project of kind dataRead, and t is a DataEdit type
  n names an 'invalid' project (see Project states in ControllersDesign)
```
- 'fsFolder': a folder of the underlying file system. 'fsFile': a file of the underlying file system.
- 'resource space': the virtual tree that Fearless code accesses through `CapDataRead` and `CapDataEdit`: the 'fsFolder' of a data project, where an 'fsFile' `x.zip` induces a 'folder' `x` holding the 'resources' of the zip (as for the `.path` of a file, see The data in CoordinatorDesign).
- 'resource': an element of the 'resource space': a 'folder' or a 'file'.

## Base

`sys.dataRead(data)` gives a `CapDataRead` and `sys.dataEdit(data)` gives a `CapDataEdit`; each method of that capability acts on the data project mounted on the DataRead type named `data.name`, as Data projects of a running main says.

Example:
```
Diary: DataEdit{}
Pub: DataRead{}
ShowDiary: Main{ sys -> ... sys.dataEdit(Diary).read(List#(`y2026`, `today.txt`)) ... sys.dataRead(Pub).paths(List#) ... }
```
`sys.dataRead(Pub)` gives the capability to read the data project mounted on `Pub`.
`sys.dataEdit(Diary)` gives the capability to read and write the data project mounted on `Diary`.
`sys.dataRead(Diary)` gives the capability to only read the data project mounted on `Diary`.
`sys.dataEdit(Pub)` is a type error: `Pub` is not a DataEdit type.

The DataRead types of a code project are known when `Coordinator.check` gives `State.compiled`: the `.dataRead` and the `.dataEdit` of the `.manifest` of `State.compiled` (see CoordinatorDesign).
The manifest of `base` lists no DataRead type.

## What the compiler records

The manifest of each package (the `.manifest` of `<pkg>.json`, see CoordinatorDesign) lists under `dataRead` the summonable DataRead types (see AttenuationDesign) of the package that are not DataEdit types, and under `dataEdit` the summonable DataEdit types of the package:
```
{
  "mains": { "hello.ShowDiary": [...] },
  "attenuators": [ "hello.NoGui" ],
  "dataRead": [ "hello.Pub" ],
  "dataEdit": [ "hello.Diary" ]
}
```

## Mounts

A data project can be mounted on several DataRead types, of one code project or of several code projects.
A broken mount in `projects.info` is well formed.

## Data projects of a running main

When the manager starts a main, the manager gives the main the 'fsFolder' and the 'lock file' of every data project mounted on a DataRead type of the project, by the qualified name of that DataRead type (the `.mounts` and the `.locks` of `Launch`, see CoordinatorDesign).
A process started by `CapProcess` gets the data projects of the process that starts it (see Processes in AttenuationDesign).
The manager checks the mounts of the project of a main only when the main starts: a data project that becomes 'invalid' while a main runs does not end the main.
A listing, a read, a write and a delete give the same results in a 'folder' induced by an 'fsFile' as in a 'folder' induced by an 'fsFolder'.
A write inside a 'folder' induced by an 'fsFile' rewrites that 'fsFile'.
A path is the list of the 'folder' names, then the name of the 'resource'.
`.paths` gives the names of the 'resources' in the 'folder' at the path, sorted; the empty path is the root 'folder'.
A 'folder' name is a 'valid segment' (see FolderShapesDesign).
A 'file' name is a 'valid file name' (see FolderShapesDesign) whose extension is not `zip` or `fearless`.
The names of a written path, joined with `/`, are at most 200 characters long.
A write replaces the 'file' at the path, or creates the 'file' together with the missing 'folders' of the path.
TODO: a delete that leaves the root 'folder' empty also deletes the root 'fsFolder'.
TODO: a write after a delete that removed the 'fsFile' `x.zip` creates the 'fsFolder' `x`, not `x.zip`: whether a write keeps the zip is to be decided.

A delete deletes a 'file', or a 'folder' with every 'resource' in the 'folder', the 'fsFiles' and 'fsFolders' whose name starts with ".", and the 'fsFiles' with extension `fearless`; then the delete deletes the 'folders' other than the root 'folder' that the delete leaves empty, and the 'fsFiles' that induce them.
These are deterministic errors:
- a path that is not valid;
- a path of `.paths`, of `.read` or of a delete that names no 'resource';
- a path of `.paths` that ends with a 'file' name;
- a path of `.read` or of a write that is empty or ends with a 'folder' name;
- a delete of the empty path.

A DataRead type with no data project given to the main, a root 'fsFolder' that can not be reached, a 'resource' that exists but can not be read or changed, and a 'folder' holding an 'entry' that breaks a 'shape rule' (see Shape rules in FolderShapesDesign) are non deterministic errors.

```
A Code project hello mounts data project diary on greet.Diary.
B The folder of diary holds diary.fearless, .notes.txt, y2026/today.txt and old.zip; old.zip holds y2025/today.txt and nothing else.
C A main of hello calls sys.dataEdit(Diary).paths(List#) and gets `old` and `y2026`.
D The main calls sys.dataEdit(Diary).paths(List#(`old`)) and gets `y2025`.
E The main calls sys.dataEdit(Diary).delete(List#(`old`, `y2025`, `today.txt`)).
F The folder of diary holds diary.fearless, .notes.txt and y2026/today.txt.

A Code project hello mounts data project diary on greet.Diary.
B The folder of diary holds diary.fearless, sub/notes.fearless and sub/notes2.txt; diary is not 'invalid'.
C A main of hello calls sys.dataEdit(Diary).paths(List#(`sub`)).
D The call returns only `notes2.txt`.
```

## Locks of data projects

Each data project has one lock, which a process holds as a 'read lock' or as the 'edit lock'.
Any number of processes hold a 'read lock' at the same time; a process holds the 'edit lock' only while no other process holds the lock.

An 'operation' is a call of a method of `CapDataRead` or `CapDataEdit`, or a read of the `CapInputCursorNode` that `.read` gave.
Each 'operation' holds the lock from its start to its end: a 'read lock' for `.paths` and for the reads, the 'edit lock' for a write and for a delete.
An 'operation' that can not take the lock waits until it can.

`sys.locked(reads, edits, f)`, a `mut` method of `System`, holds, for the whole run of `f`, a 'read lock' of the data project mounted on each DataRead type of `reads` and the 'edit lock' of the data project mounted on each DataEdit type of `edits`, and returns what `f` returns.
A data project named more than once, through several types or in both lists, is locked once: with the 'edit lock' when one of its types is in `edits`.
`.locked` takes the locks one at a time, in the order of the names of their 'lock files', and releases every lock when `f` has ended and every process that the process of `.locked` started while `f` runs has ended.
The 'lock group' of a `.locked` is the process of `.locked` while `f` runs, and every process that a process of the 'lock group' starts.
A process of the 'lock group' gets the 'lock files' of the `.locked` and their modes when it starts, and ends only after every process it started has ended.
An 'operation' of the 'lock group' on a data project of `reads` or `edits` holds the 'inner lock' of that data project instead of the lock, in the same mode.
A process runs at most one `.locked` at a time: a `.locked` waits while another `.locked` of the same process runs.
These are deterministic errors:
- a write or a delete of the 'lock group' on a data project only in `reads`;
- an 'operation' of the 'lock group' on a data project not in `reads` or `edits`;
- a `.locked` inside the `f` of a `.locked`;
- a `.locked` of a process of a 'lock group' started by `CapProcess`.

An 'autoCheck' (see Project states in ControllersDesign) of a data project holds a 'read lock'; when the lock is not free, the 'autoCheck' keeps the outcome of the previous 'autoCheck' of that data project.
Before starting a main, the manager takes the lock of every data project mounted on a DataRead type of the project, in the order of the names of their 'lock files', waiting for each lock: the 'edit lock' for a DataEdit type, a 'read lock' for the other DataRead types.
Then the manager checks the mounts of the project, starts the main, and releases those locks.

The lock is a lock of the operating system on the 'lock file' of the data project: a shared lock for a 'read lock', an exclusive lock for the 'edit lock'.
The 'lock file' of a data project is `locks/<hash>.lock` in the manager folder, where `<hash>` is the SHA-256 of the `base16` (see Info files in ControllersDesign) of the absolute path of the 'fsFolder' of the data project, in hexadecimal.
The 'inner lock' is a lock of the operating system on the 'inner lock file' `locks/<hash>_inner.lock`, next to the 'lock file'.
The 'lock file' and the 'inner lock file' hold nothing; a process creates a missing 'lock file' or 'inner lock file'.
When the manager starts, the manager deletes every file of `locks` that is not the 'lock file' or the 'inner lock file' of a registered project, unless a process holds a lock of it.

An edit leaves each 'file' as before the edit or as after the edit, also when the process ends during the edit.

## GUI

For a code project, the Data collapsible area shows each DataRead type of the project and each mount of the project.
The user mounts on a DataRead type a data project whose mount is not a broken mount, or removes a mount.
A broken mount is marked, naming what makes the mount broken.
For a data project, the Data collapsible area shows the mounts on that data project, and the user mounts that data project on a DataRead type with no mount, when that mount is not a broken mount.
Every change is a `Mount` or an `Unmount`.

## Implementation notes

An edit builds what the edit adds as a hidden 'fsFile' or 'fsFolder' `.<uuid>.tmp` in the root 'fsFolder': the new 'fsFile', the rewritten zip, or the missing 'fsFolders' holding the new 'fsFile'.
Then the edit moves the `.<uuid>.tmp` to its place with one rename.
A delete first renames what the delete deletes to a `.<uuid>.tmp` in the root 'fsFolder'.
A process that takes the 'edit lock' deletes every `.<uuid>.tmp` of the root 'fsFolder'.
A process keeps one open channel to each 'lock file' and each 'inner lock file', and a lock of its own threads in front of the lock of the operating system.
